<h1>Example 6 — <span class="mot-latin">Alleluia. Pascha nostrum</span></h1>
<p class="chapeau">
    The Alleluia of Easter Day. The same form as the Christmas one, but a wholly different
    brilliance&nbsp;: the ideal occasion to <strong>compare two jubili</strong> and hear
    what the <em>mode</em> changes in a melody.
</p>

<div class="fiche">
    <dl>
        <dt>Genre</dt><dd>Alleluia (acclamation before the Gospel)</dd>
        <dt>Occasion</dt><dd>Mass of Easter Day, and throughout Eastertide</dd>
        <dt>Text</dt><dd>1st letter to the Corinthians (1 Cor 5, 7)</dd>
        <dt>Mode</dt><dd>Mode VII (<em>sol</em> mode, bright and soaring)</dd>
        <dt>Style</dt><dd><strong>Melismatic</strong>&nbsp;: a brief verse, an ample vocalise</dd>
        <dt>In the <em>Triplex</em></dt><dd>p. 197</dd>
    </dl>
</div>

<div class="lecteur-audio">
    <span class="etiquette">Listen to the chant</span>
    <p class="note-audio">Listen first to the Christmas Alleluia, then to this one&nbsp;: mode II gathers itself in, mode VII soars.</p>
