<h2>The text and its translation</h2>
<div class="texte-latin">
    <div class="entete lat">Latin</div>
    <div class="entete fr">English</div>
    <div class="lat">Alleluia.</div>
    <div class="fr">Alleluia.</div>
    <div class="lat"><span class="chip">Verse</span> Pascha nostrum immolatus est Christus.</div>
    <div class="fr">Christ, our Passover, has been sacrificed.</div>
</div>

<div class="encadre def">
    <span class="etiquette">Reminder — the mode</span>
    <p>
        The <strong>mode</strong> is the «&nbsp;family of notes&nbsp;» in which a melody
        moves&nbsp;: its resting note, its favoured degrees, its colour. Chant has eight of
        them. Two chants of the same form and the same length can thus produce opposite
        impressions&nbsp;: that is exactly what happens between the mode II of Christmas,
        grave and inward, and the Easter mode VII, luminous and rising.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>Two Alleluias, the same mechanism</h2>
<p class="lettrine">
    Go back to the previous page and set the two chants side by side&nbsp;: you will see the
    same architecture. The word <span class="mot-latin">Alleluia</span>, its jubilus, a
    verse entrusted to the soloist, then the return of the jubilus. What Eastertide adds is
    breadth&nbsp;: the vocalise stretches further, as if the joy of Easter needed more air.
</p>

<h3>A comparison exercise</h3>
<ul>
    <li>
Count the neumes of the jubilus in both chants&nbsp;: the difference can be seen before it
is heard.
</li>
    <li>
Look for the <strong>resting note</strong> (the one the melody falls back to)&nbsp;:
<em>re</em> for Christmas, <em>sol</em> for Easter.
</li>
    <li>
Spot the <strong>quilismas</strong>&nbsp;: in the Easter ascents they are numerous — the
voice gathers momentum.
</li>
</ul>

<div class="encadre astuce">
    <span class="etiquette">The role of the old notations here</span>
    <p>
        Now is the moment to check something amusing&nbsp;: across two different jubili, the
        <strong>significative letters</strong> come back at the same strategic places — just
        before an ascent, at the top of a curve, right at the end. Laon (above) and St. Gall
        (below) do not decorate&nbsp;: they <strong>articulate</strong>.
    </p>
</div>

